{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"recursion","title":"Recursion","group":"Arrays, Bits & Recursion","order":2,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Recursion solves a problem by calling itself on a <strong>smaller instance</strong> and trusting that call's answer. The problems here — rope cutting, subsets, Tower of Hanoi, Josephus, subset sum and permutations — are the templates for backtracking and dynamic programming later in the course.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Recursion</strong> — a function that calls itself with an input strictly closer to a <strong>base case</strong>, which is answered directly. Each active call lives in its own stack frame, so the maximum depth of the recursion is also the auxiliary space.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>How a recursive call runs</h3>\n<p>Every call pushes a frame (parameters, locals, return address) on the <strong>call stack</strong>; the frame is popped when the call returns. Work written <em>before</em> the recursive call happens on the way <strong>down</strong>, work <em>after</em> it happens on the way <strong>up</strong> — the trace below shows both.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def trace(n):\n    if n == 0:                    # base case: answered without another call\n        print(\"base case reached\")\n        return\n    print(\"enter\", n)             # on the way down\n    trace(n - 1)                  # smaller instance\n    print(\"exit\", n)              # on the way up, after the sub-call finished\n\ntrace(3)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid trace(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) {                 // base case: answered without another call\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"base case reached\\n\";\n        return;\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"enter \" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";    // on the way down\n    trace(n - 1);                     // smaller instance\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"exit \" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";     // on the way up, after the sub-call finished\n}\n\nint main() { trace(3); }</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static void trace(int n) {\n        if (n == 0) {             // base case: answered without another call\n            System.out.println(\"base case reached\");\n            return;\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"enter \" + n);   // on the way down\n        trace(n - 1);                       // smaller instance\n        System.out.println(\"exit \" + n);    // on the way up, after the sub-call finished\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) { trace(3); }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function trace(n) {\n    if (n === 0) {                // base case: answered without another call\n        console.log(\"base case reached\");\n        return;\n    }\n    console.log(\"enter\", n);      // on the way down\n    trace(n - 1);                 // smaller instance\n    console.log(\"exit\", n);       // on the way up, after the sub-call finished\n}\n\ntrace(3);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>enter 3\nenter 2\nenter 1\nbase case reached\nexit 1\nexit 2\nexit 3</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Depth 4 = O(n) auxiliary space, even though the function declares no arrays</figcaption><pre><code>trace(3)                         call stack (top is the running call)\n └ trace(2)                      ┌──────────┐\n    └ trace(1)                   │ trace(0) │ ← base case, 4 frames deep\n       └ trace(0)  base case     │ trace(1) │\n                                 │ trace(2) │\n                                 │ trace(3) │\n                                 └──────────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flag\"></i>Base case first</h3><p>Without it the recursion never stops: Python raises <code>RecursionError</code> at ~1000 frames, C++/Java crash with a stack overflow.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-down\"></i>Make progress</h3><p>Every call must move strictly towards the base case (<code>n-1</code>, <code>i+1</code>, a shorter string).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brain\"></i>Trust the sub-call</h3><p>Assume <code>f(n-1)</code> is correct and only write how to build <code>f(n)</code> from it — the <em>recursive leap of faith</em>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Count the tree</h3><p>Time ≈ number of nodes in the recursion tree (branching^depth); space = its height.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scissors\"></i>Rope cutting problem</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 5, a = 2, b = 5, c = 1</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>5</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 23, a = 12, b = 9, c = 11</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>2</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 5, a = 4, b = 2, c = 6</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>-1</code></p></div>\n<p>Cut a rope of length <code>n</code> into the <strong>maximum number of pieces</strong>, each of length <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> or <code>c</code>; return <code>-1</code> if no exact division exists. For <code>n = 23</code> the best is <code>12 + 11</code> (2 pieces); for <code>n = 5</code> with <code>c = 1</code> it is five unit pieces.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>The first cut is <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> or <code>c</code>. Whichever it is, the rest of the rope is a smaller rope with the same question — so <code>maxPieces(n) = 1 + max(maxPieces(n-a), maxPieces(n-b), maxPieces(n-c))</code>. A rope of length <code>0</code> needs <code>0</code> more cuts (base case); a negative length means that cut overshoots and is reported as <code>-1</code>, which the <code>max</code> discards unless all three branches fail.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Three branches per node; a negative length is a dead branch (-1)</figcaption><pre><code>maxPieces(5)  a=2 b=5 c=1\n├── maxPieces(3)   (cut 2)\n│   ├── maxPieces(1)  → ├ (-1) → -1   ├ (-4) → -1   ├ (0) → 0     ⇒ 1\n│   ├── maxPieces(-2) → -1\n│   └── maxPieces(2)  → … ⇒ 2\n│                                          ⇒ 1 + max(1, -1, 2) = 3\n├── maxPieces(0)   (cut 5)  → 0            ⇒ 0 + 1 = 1\n└── maxPieces(4)   (cut 1)  → … ⇒ 4        ⇒ 5\n                                           answer = max(3, 1, 5) = 5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def max_pieces(n, a, b, c):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0                      # nothing left to cut: 0 more pieces\n    if n &lt; 0:\n        return -1                     # this cut overshot the rope: invalid\n    res = max(max_pieces(n - a, a, b, c),\n              max_pieces(n - b, a, b, c),\n              max_pieces(n - c, a, b, c))\n    if res == -1:\n        return -1                     # none of the three cuts can finish the rope\n    return res + 1                    # +1 for the piece cut here\n\nfor n, a, b, c in ((5, 2, 5, 1), (23, 12, 9, 11), (5, 4, 2, 6)):\n    print(f\"n={n} a={a} b={b} c={c} -&gt;\", max_pieces(n, a, b, c))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint maxPieces(int n, int a, int b, int c) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;                 // nothing left to cut: 0 more pieces\n    if (n &lt; 0) return -1;                 // this cut overshot the rope: invalid\n    int res = max({maxPieces(n - a, a, b, c),\n                   maxPieces(n - b, a, b, c),\n                   maxPieces(n - c, a, b, c)});\n    if (res == -1) return -1;             // none of the three cuts can finish the rope\n    return res + 1;                       // +1 for the piece cut here\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int tests[3][4] = {{5, 2, 5, 1}, {23, 12, 9, 11}, {5, 4, 2, 6}};\n    for (auto&amp; t : tests)\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"n=\" &lt;&lt; t[0] &lt;&lt; \" a=\" &lt;&lt; t[1] &lt;&lt; \" b=\" &lt;&lt; t[2] &lt;&lt; \" c=\" &lt;&lt; t[3]\n             &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \" &lt;&lt; maxPieces(t[0], t[1], t[2], t[3]) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int maxPieces(int n, int a, int b, int c) {\n        if (n == 0) return 0;             // nothing left to cut: 0 more pieces\n        if (n &lt; 0) return -1;             // this cut overshot the rope: invalid\n        int res = Math.max(maxPieces(n - a, a, b, c),\n                  Math.max(maxPieces(n - b, a, b, c), maxPieces(n - c, a, b, c)));\n        if (res == -1) return -1;         // none of the three cuts can finish the rope\n        return res + 1;                   // +1 for the piece cut here\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] tests = {{5, 2, 5, 1}, {23, 12, 9, 11}, {5, 4, 2, 6}};\n        for (int[] t : tests)\n            System.out.println(\"n=\" + t[0] + \" a=\" + t[1] + \" b=\" + t[2] + \" c=\" + t[3]\n                               + \" -&gt; \" + maxPieces(t[0], t[1], t[2], t[3]));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function maxPieces(n, a, b, c) {\n    if (n === 0) return 0;                // nothing left to cut: 0 more pieces\n    if (n &lt; 0) return -1;                 // this cut overshot the rope: invalid\n    const res = Math.max(maxPieces(n - a, a, b, c),\n                         maxPieces(n - b, a, b, c),\n                         maxPieces(n - c, a, b, c));\n    if (res === -1) return -1;            // none of the three cuts can finish the rope\n    return res + 1;                       // +1 for the piece cut here\n}\n\nfor (const [n, a, b, c] of [[5, 2, 5, 1], [23, 12, 9, 11], [5, 4, 2, 6]])\n    console.log(`n=${n} a=${a} b=${b} c=${c} -&gt;`, maxPieces(n, a, b, c));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>n=5 a=2 b=5 c=1 -&gt; 5\nn=23 a=12 b=9 c=11 -&gt; 2\nn=5 a=4 b=2 c=6 -&gt; -1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(3^n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) recursion depth</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Returning <code>0</code> for a negative length (instead of <code>-1</code>) silently counts an impossible cut as valid and over-reports the answer. Also note the massive overlap in the tree — <code>maxPieces(2)</code> is computed many times. Memoising on <code>n</code> turns this into the <code>O(n)</code> dynamic-programming solution in the DP topic.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Subsets of a string</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"AB\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>\"\" B A AB</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"ABC\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>\"\" C B BC A AC AB ABC</code></p></div>\n<p>Generate all <code>2^n</code> subsets (subsequences keeping the original order) of a string of distinct characters.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Walk the string one index at a time. For <code>s[i]</code> there are exactly two choices — <strong>leave it out</strong> or <strong>put it in</strong> — and the rest of the string is the same problem. When the index reaches the end, the accumulated <code>curr</code> is one finished subset. Two choices at each of <code>n</code> levels gives <code>2^n</code> leaves.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Depth n, 2^n leaves — one per subset. Exclude first, so the empty string is printed first</figcaption><pre><code>sub(\"\", 0)                                       s = \"ABC\"\n├── exclude A → sub(\"\", 1)\n│   ├── exclude B → sub(\"\", 2)\n│   │   ├── exclude C → \"\"      ✔\n│   │   └── include C → \"C\"     ✔\n│   └── include B → sub(\"B\", 2)\n│       ├── \"B\"  ✔     └── \"BC\" ✔\n└── include A → sub(\"A\", 1)\n    ├── sub(\"A\", 2)  → \"A\" ✔, \"AC\" ✔\n    └── sub(\"AB\", 2) → \"AB\" ✔, \"ABC\" ✔</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def subsets(s, curr, i, out):\n    if i == len(s):\n        out.append(curr if curr else '\"\"')    # a finished subset\n        return\n    subsets(s, curr, i + 1, out)              # exclude s[i]\n    subsets(s, curr + s[i], i + 1, out)       # include s[i]\n\nfor s in (\"AB\", \"ABC\"):\n    out = []\n    subsets(s, \"\", 0, out)\n    print(s, \"-&gt;\", \" \".join(out), f\"({len(out)} subsets)\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid subsets(const string&amp; s, string curr, int i, vector&lt;string&gt;&amp; out) {\n    if (i == (int)s.size()) {\n        out.push_back(curr.empty() ? \"\\\"\\\"\" : curr);   // a finished subset\n        return;\n    }\n    subsets(s, curr, i + 1, out);             // exclude s[i]\n    subsets(s, curr + s[i], i + 1, out);      // include s[i]\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string s : {\"AB\", \"ABC\"}) {\n        vector&lt;string&gt; out;\n        subsets(s, \"\", 0, out);\n        cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; \" -&gt;\";\n        for (const string&amp; x : out) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; x;\n        cout &lt;&lt; \" (\" &lt;&lt; out.size() &lt;&lt; \" subsets)\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void subsets(String s, String curr, int i, List&lt;String&gt; out) {\n        if (i == s.length()) {\n            out.add(curr.isEmpty() ? \"\\\"\\\"\" : curr);  // a finished subset\n            return;\n        }\n        subsets(s, curr, i + 1, out);                 // exclude s[i]\n        subsets(s, curr + s.charAt(i), i + 1, out);   // include s[i]\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String s : new String[]{\"AB\", \"ABC\"}) {\n            List&lt;String&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n            subsets(s, \"\", 0, out);\n            System.out.println(s + \" -&gt; \" + String.join(\" \", out) + \" (\" + out.size() + \" subsets)\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function subsets(s, curr, i, out) {\n    if (i === s.length) {\n        out.push(curr === \"\" ? '\"\"' : curr);  // a finished subset\n        return;\n    }\n    subsets(s, curr, i + 1, out);             // exclude s[i]\n    subsets(s, curr + s[i], i + 1, out);      // include s[i]\n}\n\nfor (const s of [\"AB\", \"ABC\"]) {\n    const out = [];\n    subsets(s, \"\", 0, out);\n    console.log(s, \"-&gt;\", out.join(\" \"), `(${out.length} subsets)`);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>AB -&gt; \"\" B A AB (4 subsets)\nABC -&gt; \"\" C B BC A AC AB ABC (8 subsets)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(2^n · n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) recursion depth (plus the output)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Swapping the two recursive calls (include first) lists <code>ABC AB AC A BC B C \"\"</code> instead — the set of subsets is the same, only the order changes. The iterative bitmask version of this problem is in Bit Magic → <em>Power set using bitmasks</em>. With repeated characters this prints duplicate subsets; sort the string and skip equal neighbours to avoid that.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-3\"></i>Tower of Hanoi</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 1</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>Move disc 1 from A to C</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 2</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>Move disc 1 from A to B / Move disc 2 from A to C / Move disc 1 from B to C</code></p></div>\n<p>Move <code>n</code> discs from peg <strong>A</strong> (source) to peg <strong>C</strong> (destination) using peg <strong>B</strong> (auxiliary), under three rules:</p>\n<ol><li>Only <strong>one disc</strong> moves at a time.</li><li>A <strong>larger disc may never sit on a smaller</strong> one.</li><li>Only the <strong>top disc</strong> of a peg can be moved.</li></ol>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>n = 3: the whole tower must end on C in the same order</figcaption><pre><code>    A          B          C                 A          B          C\n    |          |          |                 |          |          |\n   ═╪═         |          |      →          |          |         ═╪═\n  ══╪══        |          |                 |          |        ══╪══\n ═══╪═══       |          |                 |          |       ═══╪═══\n────┴──────────┴──────────┴──          ────┴──────────┴──────────┴──</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>To move <code>n</code> discs A → C, the biggest disc <code>n</code> can only move when the other <code>n-1</code> discs are out of the way on B. So: move <code>n-1</code> discs A → B (using C as spare), move disc <code>n</code> A → C, then move the <code>n-1</code> discs B → C (using A as spare). The recursion is the same problem with the pegs <strong>relabelled</strong> — that is why the function takes the three pegs as parameters.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>TOH(n-1, A, C, B)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Park the top <code>n-1</code> discs on the auxiliary peg B, using C as the spare.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Move disc <code>n</code> from A to C</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The largest disc makes its single move onto the empty destination.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>TOH(n-1, B, A, C)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Bring the <code>n-1</code> discs from B onto C, using A as the spare.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"formula\">moves(n) = 2 · moves(n−1) + 1 = 2^n − 1 (3 discs → 7, 10 discs → 1023, 64 discs → 1.8 × 10^19)</div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def toh(n, src, aux, dst):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0                              # no discs: no moves\n    moves = toh(n - 1, src, dst, aux)         # park n-1 discs on the auxiliary peg\n    print(f\"Move disc {n} from {src} to {dst}\")\n    moves += 1 + toh(n - 1, aux, src, dst)    # bring them onto the destination\n    return moves\n\nfor n in (2, 3):\n    print(\"n =\", n)\n    print(\"moves:\", toh(n, \"A\", \"B\", \"C\"))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint toh(int n, char src, char aux, char dst) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;                         // no discs: no moves\n    int moves = toh(n - 1, src, dst, aux);        // park n-1 discs on the auxiliary peg\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Move disc \" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \" from \" &lt;&lt; src &lt;&lt; \" to \" &lt;&lt; dst &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    moves += 1 + toh(n - 1, aux, src, dst);       // bring them onto the destination\n    return moves;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (int n : {2, 3}) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"n = \" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n        int moves = toh(n, 'A', 'B', 'C');      // run first: the moves print inside\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"moves: \" &lt;&lt; moves &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int toh(int n, char src, char aux, char dst) {\n        if (n == 0) return 0;                     // no discs: no moves\n        int moves = toh(n - 1, src, dst, aux);    // park n-1 discs on the auxiliary peg\n        System.out.println(\"Move disc \" + n + \" from \" + src + \" to \" + dst);\n        moves += 1 + toh(n - 1, aux, src, dst);   // bring them onto the destination\n        return moves;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int n : new int[]{2, 3}) {\n            System.out.println(\"n = \" + n);\n            System.out.println(\"moves: \" + toh(n, 'A', 'B', 'C'));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function toh(n, src, aux, dst) {\n    if (n === 0) return 0;                        // no discs: no moves\n    let moves = toh(n - 1, src, dst, aux);        // park n-1 discs on the auxiliary peg\n    console.log(`Move disc ${n} from ${src} to ${dst}`);\n    moves += 1 + toh(n - 1, aux, src, dst);       // bring them onto the destination\n    return moves;\n}\n\nfor (const n of [2, 3]) {\n    console.log(\"n =\", n);\n    console.log(\"moves:\", toh(n, \"A\", \"B\", \"C\"));\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>n = 2\nMove disc 1 from A to B\nMove disc 2 from A to C\nMove disc 1 from B to C\nmoves: 3\nn = 3\nMove disc 1 from A to C\nMove disc 2 from A to B\nMove disc 1 from C to B\nMove disc 3 from A to C\nMove disc 1 from B to A\nMove disc 2 from B to C\nMove disc 1 from A to C\nmoves: 7</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(2^n) moves</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) recursion depth</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>The classic slip is the <strong>peg order</strong> in the two recursive calls. With the signature <code>TOH(n, source, auxiliary, destination)</code> the first call is <code>TOH(n-1, A, C, B)</code> (destination becomes the spare) and the second is <code>TOH(n-1, B, A, C)</code> (source becomes the spare). Using <code>n == 1</code> as the base case also works; <code>n == 0</code> avoids a special-cased print.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-users\"></i>Josephus problem (elimination game)</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 7, k = 3</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>3</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 8, k = 2</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>0</code></p></div>\n<p><code>n</code> people stand in a circle numbered <code>0 … n-1</code>. Starting at <code>0</code>, every <strong>kth</strong> person is eliminated and the count restarts from the next person. Which position survives?</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>After the first elimination (<code>k-1</code> is removed), the circle has <code>n-1</code> people and the count restarts at position <code>k</code>. If we <strong>renumber</strong> so that position <code>k</code> becomes <code>0</code>, the remaining game is exactly <code>josephus(n-1, k)</code>. Its survivor <code>j</code> in the renumbered circle sits at <code>(j + k) % n</code> in the original one — hence <code>jos(n, k) = (jos(n-1, k) + k) % n</code>, with <code>jos(1, k) = 0</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Unwinding the recursion from the base case up</figcaption><pre><code>jos(1,3) = 0\njos(2,3) = (0 + 3) % 2 = 1\njos(3,3) = (1 + 3) % 3 = 1\njos(4,3) = (1 + 3) % 4 = 0\njos(5,3) = (0 + 3) % 5 = 3\njos(6,3) = (3 + 3) % 6 = 0\njos(7,3) = (0 + 3) % 7 = 3     ← survivor for n = 7, k = 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def josephus(n, k):\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0                          # one person left: position 0 survives\n    return (josephus(n - 1, k) + k) % n   # shift the smaller game's answer back by k\n\nfor n, k in ((7, 3), (8, 2), (5, 2)):\n    print(f\"n={n} k={k} -&gt;\", josephus(n, k))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint josephus(int n, int k) {\n    if (n == 1) return 0;                     // one person left: position 0 survives\n    return (josephus(n - 1, k) + k) % n;      // shift the smaller game's answer back by k\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int tests[3][2] = {{7, 3}, {8, 2}, {5, 2}};\n    for (auto&amp; t : tests)\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"n=\" &lt;&lt; t[0] &lt;&lt; \" k=\" &lt;&lt; t[1] &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \" &lt;&lt; josephus(t[0], t[1]) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int josephus(int n, int k) {\n        if (n == 1) return 0;                 // one person left: position 0 survives\n        return (josephus(n - 1, k) + k) % n;  // shift the smaller game's answer back by k\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] tests = {{7, 3}, {8, 2}, {5, 2}};\n        for (int[] t : tests)\n            System.out.println(\"n=\" + t[0] + \" k=\" + t[1] + \" -&gt; \" + josephus(t[0], t[1]));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function josephus(n, k) {\n    if (n === 1) return 0;                    // one person left: position 0 survives\n    return (josephus(n - 1, k) + k) % n;      // shift the smaller game's answer back by k\n}\n\nfor (const [n, k] of [[7, 3], [8, 2], [5, 2]])\n    console.log(`n=${n} k=${k} -&gt;`, josephus(n, k));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>n=7 k=3 -&gt; 3\nn=8 k=2 -&gt; 0\nn=5 k=2 -&gt; 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) recursion depth — or O(1) with a loop</span></div>\n<p>To <strong>see</strong> the eliminations, simulate the circle with a list: the next victim is <code>(idx + k - 1) % size</code> and the count resumes at that index.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def simulate(n, k):\n    people = list(range(n))\n    idx = 0\n    while len(people) &gt; 1:\n        idx = (idx + k - 1) % len(people)     # count k people from idx, wrapping around\n        removed = people.pop(idx)             # the next person now sits at idx\n        print(\"remove\", removed, \"-&gt; left:\", \" \".join(map(str, people)))\n    print(\"survivor:\", people[0])\n\nsimulate(7, 3)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid simulate(int n, int k) {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; people;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) people.push_back(i);\n    int idx = 0;\n    while (people.size() &gt; 1) {\n        int size = people.size();\n        idx = (idx + k - 1) % size;               // count k people from idx, wrapping around\n        int removed = people[idx];\n        people.erase(people.begin() + idx);       // the next person now sits at idx\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"remove \" &lt;&lt; removed &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; left:\";\n        for (int x : people) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; x;\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"survivor: \" &lt;&lt; people[0] &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() { simulate(7, 3); }</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void simulate(int n, int k) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; people = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) people.add(i);\n        int idx = 0;\n        while (people.size() &gt; 1) {\n            idx = (idx + k - 1) % people.size();  // count k people from idx, wrapping around\n            int removed = people.remove(idx);     // the next person now sits at idx\n            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n            for (int x : people) sb.append(' ').append(x);\n            System.out.println(\"remove \" + removed + \" -&gt; left:\" + sb);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"survivor: \" + people.get(0));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) { simulate(7, 3); }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function simulate(n, k) {\n    const people = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) =&gt; i);\n    let idx = 0;\n    while (people.length &gt; 1) {\n        idx = (idx + k - 1) % people.length;      // count k people from idx, wrapping around\n        const [removed] = people.splice(idx, 1);  // the next person now sits at idx\n        console.log(\"remove\", removed, \"-&gt; left:\", people.join(\" \"));\n    }\n    console.log(\"survivor:\", people[0]);\n}\n\nsimulate(7, 3);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>remove 2 -&gt; left: 0 1 3 4 5 6\nremove 5 -&gt; left: 0 1 3 4 6\nremove 1 -&gt; left: 0 3 4 6\nremove 6 -&gt; left: 0 3 4\nremove 4 -&gt; left: 0 3\nremove 0 -&gt; left: 3\nsurvivor: 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n²) with list deletion, O(n · k) with a linked circle</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Mixing up <strong>0-based and 1-based</strong> numbering. The formula above returns a 0-based position; if the people are numbered <code>1 … n</code> the survivor is <code>jos(n, k) + 1</code> (for <code>n = 7, k = 3</code> that is <code>4</code>). Also <code>people.remove(idx)</code> in Java must receive an <code>int</code>, not an <code>Integer</code> — <code>remove(Integer)</code> removes by <em>value</em>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-coin\"></i>Subset sum (count the subsets)</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 5, 2, 3, 6], sum = 8</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>2</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 20, 15], sum = 0</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>1</code></p></div>\n<p>Count the subsets of <code>arr</code> whose elements add up to <code>sum</code>. For <code>{10, 5, 2, 3, 6}</code> and <code>8</code> there are two: <code>{5, 3}</code> and <code>{2, 6}</code>. For <code>sum = 0</code> the empty subset always counts, so the answer is at least <code>1</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Look at the <strong>last</strong> element <code>arr[n-1]</code>. Either it is left out — count the subsets of the first <code>n-1</code> elements that reach <code>sum</code> — or it is taken — count those that reach <code>sum - arr[n-1]</code>. Add the two. With no elements left (<code>n == 0</code>) there is exactly one subset, the empty one, and it works only if the remaining <code>sum</code> is <code>0</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Each leaf with sum 0 is one valid subset</figcaption><pre><code>count(n=3, sum=3)   arr = {1, 2, 3}\n├── exclude 3 → count(2, 3)\n│   ├── exclude 2 → count(1, 3) → count(0,3)=0, count(0,2)=0          ⇒ 0\n│   └── include 2 → count(1, 1) → count(0,1)=0, count(0,0)=1  {1,2}  ⇒ 1\n└── include 3 → count(2, 0)\n    ├── exclude 2 → count(1, 0) → count(0,0)=1  {3}, count(0,-1)=0   ⇒ 1\n    └── include 2 → count(1,-2) → 0                                   ⇒ 0\n                                                               total = 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def count_subsets(arr, n, target):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1 if target == 0 else 0            # empty subset: valid only if nothing is left to reach\n    return (count_subsets(arr, n - 1, target)                 # exclude arr[n-1]\n            + count_subsets(arr, n - 1, target - arr[n - 1]))  # include arr[n-1]\n\nfor arr, target in (([10, 5, 2, 3, 6], 8), ([10, 20, 15], 0), ([1, 2, 3], 3)):\n    print(\" \".join(map(str, arr)), \"| sum\", target, \"-&gt;\", count_subsets(arr, len(arr), target))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint countSubsets(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; arr, int n, int target) {\n    if (n == 0) return target == 0 ? 1 : 0;       // empty subset: valid only if nothing is left to reach\n    return countSubsets(arr, n - 1, target)                   // exclude arr[n-1]\n         + countSubsets(arr, n - 1, target - arr[n - 1]);     // include arr[n-1]\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;pair&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;, int&gt;&gt; tests = {{{10, 5, 2, 3, 6}, 8}, {{10, 20, 15}, 0}, {{1, 2, 3}, 3}};\n    for (auto&amp; [arr, target] : tests) {\n        for (int x : arr) cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \" \";\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"| sum \" &lt;&lt; target &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \" &lt;&lt; countSubsets(arr, arr.size(), target) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int countSubsets(int[] arr, int n, int target) {\n        if (n == 0) return target == 0 ? 1 : 0;   // empty subset: valid only if nothing is left to reach\n        return countSubsets(arr, n - 1, target)               // exclude arr[n-1]\n             + countSubsets(arr, n - 1, target - arr[n - 1]); // include arr[n-1]\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] arrays = {{10, 5, 2, 3, 6}, {10, 20, 15}, {1, 2, 3}};\n        int[] targets = {8, 0, 3};\n        for (int t = 0; t &lt; arrays.length; t++) {\n            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n            for (int x : arrays[t]) sb.append(x).append(' ');\n            System.out.println(sb + \"| sum \" + targets[t] + \" -&gt; \"\n                               + countSubsets(arrays[t], arrays[t].length, targets[t]));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function countSubsets(arr, n, target) {\n    if (n === 0) return target === 0 ? 1 : 0;     // empty subset: valid only if nothing is left to reach\n    return countSubsets(arr, n - 1, target)                   // exclude arr[n-1]\n         + countSubsets(arr, n - 1, target - arr[n - 1]);     // include arr[n-1]\n}\n\nfor (const [arr, target] of [[[10, 5, 2, 3, 6], 8], [[10, 20, 15], 0], [[1, 2, 3], 3]])\n    console.log(arr.join(\" \"), \"| sum\", target, \"-&gt;\", countSubsets(arr, arr.length, target));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 5 2 3 6 | sum 8 -&gt; 2\n10 20 15 | sum 0 -&gt; 1\n1 2 3 | sum 3 -&gt; 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(2^n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) recursion depth</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Pruning <code>target &lt; 0</code> (return <code>0</code> early) is safe only when all elements are non-negative. The same recurrence, memoised on <code>(n, target)</code>, is the <code>O(n · sum)</code> subset-sum DP; replacing <code>+</code> with <code>or</code> answers the <em>decision</em> version (does any subset reach the sum?).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Print all permutations of a string</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"AB\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>AB BA</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"ABC\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>ABC ACB BAC BCA CBA CAB</code></p></div>\n<p>Print every arrangement of the characters of <code>s</code> — there are <code>n!</code> of them for <code>n</code> distinct characters.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Fix position <code>i</code> by trying <strong>every</strong> character from <code>i</code> to the end in that slot (swap it in), recursively permute the rest <code>i+1 … n-1</code>, then <strong>swap it back</strong> so the next candidate starts from the original arrangement. The swap-back is what makes this <em>backtracking</em>: the shared array is restored after every choice.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>n choices at level 0, n−1 at level 1, … → n! leaves. Note the order is not alphabetical</figcaption><pre><code>permute(ABC, i=0)\n├── swap(0,0) → A|BC  permute(i=1)\n│                ├── swap(1,1) → AB|C → ABC ✔\n│                └── swap(1,2) → AC|B → ACB ✔   (swap back → ABC)\n├── swap(0,1) → B|AC  permute(i=1)\n│                ├── BAC ✔\n│                └── BCA ✔                       (swap back → ABC)\n└── swap(0,2) → C|BA  permute(i=1)\n                 ├── CBA ✔\n                 └── CAB ✔                       (swap back → ABC)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def permute(chars, i, out):\n    n = len(chars)\n    if i == n - 1:\n        out.append(\"\".join(chars))            # every position fixed: one permutation\n        return\n    for j in range(i, n):\n        chars[i], chars[j] = chars[j], chars[i]   # choose chars[j] for position i\n        permute(chars, i + 1, out)\n        chars[i], chars[j] = chars[j], chars[i]   # backtrack: undo the swap\n\nfor s in (\"AB\", \"ABC\"):\n    out = []\n    permute(list(s), 0, out)\n    print(s, \"-&gt;\", \" \".join(out))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid permute(string&amp; s, int i, vector&lt;string&gt;&amp; out) {\n    int n = s.size();\n    if (i == n - 1) {\n        out.push_back(s);                     // every position fixed: one permutation\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int j = i; j &lt; n; j++) {\n        swap(s[i], s[j]);                     // choose s[j] for position i\n        permute(s, i + 1, out);\n        swap(s[i], s[j]);                     // backtrack: undo the swap\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string s : {\"AB\", \"ABC\"}) {\n        vector&lt;string&gt; out;\n        string work = s;\n        permute(work, 0, out);\n        cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; \" -&gt;\";\n        for (const string&amp; p : out) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; p;\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void permute(char[] s, int i, List&lt;String&gt; out) {\n        int n = s.length;\n        if (i == n - 1) {\n            out.add(new String(s));           // every position fixed: one permutation\n            return;\n        }\n        for (int j = i; j &lt; n; j++) {\n            char t = s[i]; s[i] = s[j]; s[j] = t;   // choose s[j] for position i\n            permute(s, i + 1, out);\n            t = s[i]; s[i] = s[j]; s[j] = t;        // backtrack: undo the swap\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String s : new String[]{\"AB\", \"ABC\"}) {\n            List&lt;String&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n            permute(s.toCharArray(), 0, out);\n            System.out.println(s + \" -&gt; \" + String.join(\" \", out));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function permute(chars, i, out) {\n    const n = chars.length;\n    if (i === n - 1) {\n        out.push(chars.join(\"\"));             // every position fixed: one permutation\n        return;\n    }\n    for (let j = i; j &lt; n; j++) {\n        [chars[i], chars[j]] = [chars[j], chars[i]];   // choose chars[j] for position i\n        permute(chars, i + 1, out);\n        [chars[i], chars[j]] = [chars[j], chars[i]];   // backtrack: undo the swap\n    }\n}\n\nfor (const s of [\"AB\", \"ABC\"]) {\n    const out = [];\n    permute([...s], 0, out);\n    console.log(s, \"-&gt;\", out.join(\" \"));\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>AB -&gt; AB BA\nABC -&gt; ABC ACB BAC BCA CBA CAB</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n! · n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) recursion depth, swapping in place</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Forgetting the <strong>second swap</strong> corrupts the array for the next <code>j</code> and produces duplicates and missing permutations. The in-place swap uses no extra array, but the auxiliary space is still <code>O(n)</code> because of the recursion depth — not <code>O(1)</code>. With repeated characters (<code>\"AAB\"</code>) this prints duplicates; skip a <code>j</code> whose character was already tried at this position, or use a frequency map.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Recursion trees at a glance</h3>\n<p>The cost of a recursive solution is read off its tree: <strong>time</strong> is roughly the number of nodes (branching factor to the power of the depth), <strong>auxiliary space</strong> is the height. Overlapping subproblems (the same node appearing many times) are the signal to memoise.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Branching</th><th>Depth</th><th>Time</th><th>Aux space</th><th>Overlap?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rope cutting</td><td>3</td><td><code>n / min(a,b,c)</code></td><td><code>O(3^n)</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code></td><td>yes → DP <code>O(n)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Subsets of a string</td><td>2</td><td><code>n</code></td><td><code>O(2^n · n)</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code></td><td>no — every leaf is a distinct answer</td></tr><tr><td>Tower of Hanoi</td><td>2</td><td><code>n</code></td><td><code>O(2^n)</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code></td><td>no — every move is output</td></tr><tr><td>Josephus</td><td>1</td><td><code>n</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code> (loop: <code>O(1)</code>)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Subset sum count</td><td>2</td><td><code>n</code></td><td><code>O(2^n)</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code></td><td>yes → DP <code>O(n · sum)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Permutations</td><td><code>n, n-1, …, 1</code></td><td><code>n</code></td><td><code>O(n! · n)</code></td><td><code>O(n)</code></td><td>no — every leaf is a distinct answer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Depth limits</div><p>Each language limits recursion depth: Python stops at about <strong>1000</strong> frames (<code>sys.setrecursionlimit</code> raises it, but the C stack still overflows around tens of thousands), Java and C++ overflow their thread stack at roughly <code>10^4</code>–<code>10^5</code> frames depending on frame size, Node around <code>10^4</code>. A linear recursion over <code>n = 10^5</code> elements must be rewritten as a loop or use an explicit stack.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are the two things every recursive function needs?</summary><div><p>A <strong>base case</strong> that returns without recursing, and a <strong>recursive case</strong> whose argument is strictly closer to the base case. Missing or unreachable base cases give infinite recursion; a recursive case that does not shrink the input (e.g. <code>f(n)</code> calling <code>f(n)</code>) does the same.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is tail recursion, and does it help?</summary><div><p>A call is tail-recursive when the recursive call is the very last action, with nothing left to do after it returns — <code>jos</code> above is <em>not</em>, because it still adds <code>k</code> and takes <code>% n</code> after the call returns. C++ compilers optimise tail calls at <code>-O2</code>; Python and Java never do, JavaScript engines in practice do not either. So in those languages tail recursion is a style, not a space saving — convert to a loop when depth matters.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is Tower of Hanoi O(2^n) and not O(n)? There is only one print per call.</summary><div><p>Because there are <code>2^n − 1</code> calls that print: <code>moves(n) = 2·moves(n−1) + 1</code>. The recursion tree is a full binary tree of height <code>n</code>, and every node does <code>O(1)</code> work, so the total is the number of nodes. A single <code>print</code> per call does not mean a linear number of calls.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you turn an exponential recursion into a fast one?</summary><div><p>Check whether the same subproblem appears many times in the tree (rope cutting: <code>maxPieces(2)</code> is recomputed; subset sum: <code>(n, target)</code> repeats). If so, store each result the first time it is computed — <strong>memoisation</strong> (top-down DP) — or fill a table from the base cases up (bottom-up DP). If every leaf is a distinct required answer (subsets, permutations, Hanoi) the output itself is exponential and no algorithm can be faster.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Recursion vs iteration — when to pick which?</summary><div><p>Recursion wins when the problem is naturally a tree of choices (subsets, permutations, Hanoi, tree traversals): the call stack keeps the partial state for you and the code mirrors the definition. Iteration wins for linear problems (sum, factorial, Josephus) where recursion only adds <code>O(n)</code> stack and the risk of overflow. Any recursion can be made iterative with an explicit stack; it is just more code.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does the Josephus formula (jos(n-1, k) + k) % n actually mean?</summary><div><p>After the first kill the circle is one person smaller and the count restarts at position <code>k</code> (0-based). Renumber that person as <code>0</code> and the rest is a smaller instance of the game. Its survivor <code>j</code> corresponds to <code>j + k</code> in the original numbering — the <code>% n</code> wraps around the circle. Unwinding from <code>jos(1) = 0</code> gives the answer in <code>O(n)</code> with no simulation.</p></div></details></section>"}